Document (dev.stxt.website): Tools | STXT

	Metadata:
		Last modif: 2026-09-17
		Description: STXT tools: browser playground, VS Code extension, command line and TypeScript, Java and Python libraries, all implementing the same five specifications.

	Header: Tools

	Assert >>
		Every tool on this page implements the same five specifications and reports the same
		error codes.

	Content >>
		Depending on the use:

		| Use                                            | Tool                                                       |
		|------------------------------------------------|------------------------------------------------------------|
		| Try the language without installing anything   | The [playground](https://play.stxt.dev)                    |
		| Write documents regularly                      | The VS Code extension                                      |
		| Validate from a script, a `Makefile` or CI     | The `stxt` command line                                    |
		| Read or generate @STXT@ from a program         | A library: TypeScript, Java or Python                      |

	Subheader: Playground

	Content >>
		[play.stxt.dev](https://play.stxt.dev) is an editor in the browser: several documents
		at once, their schemas or templates next to them, syntax and validation errors while
		editing, grammar-driven autocompletion and hover with the definition of each node.

		* No installation and no account; the work is kept in the browser itself.
		* It includes a seed of example documents.
		* The whole workspace can be shared with a link that carries the content inside.

	Subheader: VS Code extension

	Content >>
		The official extension is
		[STXT Language (`stxt-lang.stxt`)](https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=stxt-lang.stxt).
		It is installed from the extensions view, searching for `stxt`, or from the terminal:

	Listing >>
		code --install-extension stxt-lang.stxt

	Content >>
		Features:

		* Syntax errors in real time, and schema errors; a namespace no level defines is
		  flagged with `SCHEMA_NOT_FOUND` as a warning.
		* Semantic highlighting.
		* Completion with the nodes the schema allows and the values of an `ENUM`.
		* Hover information: the description given by the definition, the type and, in an `ENUM`, its values.
		* Go to definition (F12): from a node to the `Node:` of its schema, or to its
		  line inside the `Structure` of its template.
		* Document formatting.
		* Schema and template resolution from the `.stxt/` directories of the project,
		  the user and the system, as described in the [discovery reference](stxt-discovery-ref).

	Subheader: Command line

	Content >>
		The `stxt` command is published on npm as
		[`@stxt-lang/cli`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@stxt-lang/cli) and requires
		Node 20 or newer.

	Listing >>
		npm install -g @stxt-lang/cli
		stxt --version

	Content >>
		Without installing, through `npx`:

	Listing >>
		npx @stxt-lang/cli validate --recursive docs/

	Content >>
		The commands:

		| Command          | What it does                                                                  |
		|------------------|-------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
		| `stxt validate`  | Parses and validates one or more files or directories; no output when all pass |
		| `stxt format`    | Reformats (`--tabs` / `--spaces`); `--check` for CI, `--write` to save        |
		| `stxt describe`  | Prints the logical tree of a document as JSON (STXT-TREE-SPEC)                |
		| `stxt schemas`   | Shows which schemas and templates apply in a directory, and from which level  |
		| `stxt install`   | Installs a definition into `./.stxt`, `~/.stxt` or the system level           |

		The exit code distinguishes document errors (`1`) from command misuse (`2`). No
		command rewrites files without an explicit option. The options, outputs and exit
		codes are in the [command-line reference](tools-cli).

	Subheader: Libraries

	Content >>
		There are three official implementations: TypeScript/JavaScript, Java and Python. All
		three pass the [conformance kit](https://github.com/stxt-lang/stxt-lang/tree/master/conformance), which also serves
		to check a port to another language.

	Subsubheader: TypeScript / JavaScript

	Content >>
		[`@stxt-lang/core`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@stxt-lang/core) on npm. It is
		the parser the extension, the command line and the playground use. The guide is in
		[the TypeScript library](tools-typescript).

	Listing >>
		npm install @stxt-lang/core

	Content >>
		A minimal example:

	TypeScript >>
		import { Parser, InlineNode } from '@stxt-lang/core';

		const result = new Parser().parseResult(text);
		for (const error of result.getErrors()) {
			console.error(`line ${error.line} [${error.code}]: ${error.message}`);
		}
		const root = result.getNodes()[0];
		if (root instanceof InlineNode) {
			console.log(root.getChild('Title')?.getText());
		}

	Subsubheader: Java

	Content >>
		[`dev.stxt:stxt-core`](https://central.sonatype.com/artifact/dev.stxt/stxt-core)
		on Maven Central. Java 17 or newer; automatic module `dev.stxt`. The guide is in
		[the Java library](tools-java).

	Xml >>
		<dependency>
		    <groupId>dev.stxt</groupId>
		    <artifactId>stxt-core</artifactId>
		    <version>1.0.3</version>
		</dependency>

	Content >>
		Or with Gradle:

	Listing >>
		implementation 'dev.stxt:stxt-core:1.0.3'

	Subsubheader: Python

	Content >>
		[`stxt`](https://pypi.org/project/stxt/) on PyPI. Pure Python, 3.10 or newer. The
		guide is in [the Python library](tools-python).

	Listing >>
		pip install stxt

	Content >>
		A minimal example:

	Python >>
		from stxt import Parser, InlineNode

		result = Parser().parse_result(text)
		for error in result.get_errors():
		    print(f"line {error.line} [{error.code}]: {error.message}")
		root = result.get_nodes()[0]
		if isinstance(root, InlineNode):
		    print(root.get_child("Title").get_text())

	Subheader: Source code

	Content >>
		The whole ecosystem lives in the
		[stxt-lang](https://github.com/stxt-lang) organization on GitHub, under the MIT
		license:

		| Repository                                                      | Contents                                              |
		|-----------------------------------------------------------------|-------------------------------------------------------|
		| [stxt-lang](https://github.com/stxt-lang/stxt-lang)               | This site: the specifications, the conformance kit and the example corpus |
		| [stxt-js](https://github.com/stxt-lang/stxt-js)                 | `@stxt-lang/core`, the TypeScript library             |
		| [stxt-java](https://github.com/stxt-lang/stxt-java)             | `dev.stxt:stxt-core`, the Java library                |
		| [stxt-python](https://github.com/stxt-lang/stxt-python)         | `stxt`, the Python library                            |
		| [stxt-cli](https://github.com/stxt-lang/stxt-cli)               | `@stxt-lang/cli`, the command line                    |
		| [stxt-vscode](https://github.com/stxt-lang/stxt-vscode)         | The VS Code extension                                 |
		| [stxt-play](https://github.com/stxt-lang/stxt-play)             | The playground                                        |

		Questions and ideas about the language go to the
		[discussions](https://github.com/orgs/stxt-lang/discussions) of the organization; bugs,
		to the issues of the repository of the tool concerned.
